partition "**VECTOR DATABASE TYPES & CATEGORIES**" {

:**Vector Database Categories**
----
**1. PURPOSE-BUILT VECTOR DATABASES**
* Designed specifically for vectors
* Optimized for similarity search
* Examples:
  - Pinecone (Cloud, Managed)
  - Weaviate (Open-source, Hybrid)
  - Qdrant (Open-source, Rust)
  - Milvus (Open-source, Distributed)
  - ChromaDB (Open-source, Python)
  - Vespa (Open-source, Enterprise)

**2. VECTOR SEARCH LIBRARIES**
* In-memory/local operations
* No server required
* Examples:
  - FAISS (Facebook AI, C++)
  - Annoy (Spotify, C++)
  - HNSW (Hierarchical NSW)
  - ScaNN (Google)
  - NGT (Yahoo Japan)

**3. EXTENDED DATABASES**
* Traditional DB + vector support
* Multi-modal capabilities
* Examples:
  - PostgreSQL + pgvector
  - Elasticsearch + Vector Search
  - Redis + RediSearch
  - MongoDB Atlas Vector Search
  - Cassandra + SAI
  - Neo4j Vector Index

**4. CLOUD-MANAGED SERVICES**
* Fully managed solutions
* Examples:
  - Pinecone
  - Azure AI Search
  - AWS OpenSearch
  - Google Vertex AI Matching Engine
  - Cloudflare Vectorize;

:**Key Characteristics Comparison**
----
**Performance:**
* Pinecone: High throughput, low latency
* Weaviate: Balanced, hybrid search
* Qdrant: High performance, Rust-based
* Milvus: Distributed, scalable
* FAISS: Fastest in-memory
* pgvector: Good for existing Postgres

**Scalability:**
* Milvus: Billions of vectors
* Pinecone: Auto-scaling
* Weaviate: Horizontal scaling
* Qdrant: Distributed architecture
* FAISS: Single-machine limit
* ChromaDB: Moderate scale

**Features:**
* Weaviate: Hybrid search, GraphQL
* Qdrant: Filtering, payload
* Milvus: GPU support, partitions
* pgvector: SQL integration
* Elasticsearch: Full-text + vector

**Open-Source:**
* Open: Weaviate, Qdrant, Milvus,
  ChromaDB, FAISS, pgvector
* Closed: Pinecone
* Hybrid: Elasticsearch, MongoDB;

}

partition "**VECTOR STORAGE ARCHITECTURE**" {

:**Vector Data Model**
----
**Core Components:**

**1. VECTOR (Embedding)**
* Numerical representation
* Fixed dimensions: 128, 384, 768, 1536, etc.
* Data type: float32, float16, int8
* Example: (0.23, -0.45, 0.67, ...)
* Memory: dim × bytes per value
  - 768 dim × 4 bytes = 3KB per vector

**2. VECTOR ID**
* Unique identifier
* UUID or integer
* Links vector to original data
* Index key

**3. METADATA (Payload)**
* Additional attributes
* Structured data:
  - Document ID
  - Title, author, date
  - Categories, tags
  - Custom fields (JSON)
* Used for filtering
* Enables hybrid search

**4. ORIGINAL CONTENT (Optional)**
* Source text/data
* May be stored separately
* Retrieved after similarity match;

:**Storage Structure**
----
**Physical Storage:**

**Embedding Storage:**
* Dense vector arrays
* Optimized memory layout
* Column-oriented storage
* Compression options:
  - Quantization (reduce precision)
  - Product Quantization (PQ)
  - Scalar Quantization (SQ)
  - Binary Quantization

**Metadata Storage:**
* Key-value store
* Inverted indices (for filtering)
* Document store (JSON/BSON)
* SQL tables (for relational DBs)

**Index Structure:**
* Specialized data structures
* Trade-off: speed vs accuracy vs memory
* Multiple index types per database;

:**Data Organization**
----
**Collections/Tables:**
* Group related vectors
* Separate namespaces
* Schema definition
* Per-collection configuration

**Partitions/Shards:**
* Horizontal splitting
* Based on key/hash
* Enables distributed storage
* Parallel processing

**Segments:**
* Data chunks
* Immutable blocks
* Background compaction
* Version control;

}

partition "**INDEXING ALGORITHMS & STRUCTURES**" {

:**Index Types**
----
**1. FLAT INDEX (Brute Force)**
* Exhaustive search
* Compare with all vectors
* 100% accuracy (recall)
* Time: O(n × d)
* Use: Small datasets (< 10K)
* No preprocessing needed
* High latency for large scale

**2. IVF (Inverted File Index)**
* Clustering-based
* Divide into clusters (centroids)
* Search nearest clusters only
* Steps:
  1. k-means clustering (build)
  2. Query: find nearest centroids
  3. Search within clusters
* Parameters:
  - nlist: number of clusters
  - nprobe: clusters to search
* Trade-off: speed vs accuracy
* Used by: FAISS, Milvus

**3. HNSW (Hierarchical NSW)**
* Graph-based algorithm
* Multi-layer graph structure
* Greedy search through layers
* High accuracy + fast search
* Parameters:
  - M: max connections per node
  - efConstruction: build quality
  - efSearch: search quality
* Memory intensive
* Used by: Qdrant, Weaviate, FAISS

**4. LSH (Locality-Sensitive Hashing)**
* Hash similar vectors to same buckets
* Probabilistic algorithm
* Fast but lower accuracy
* Good for high dimensions
* Used by: older systems

**5. ANNOY (Approximate Nearest)**
* Tree-based (binary trees)
* Random projection trees
* Multiple trees for accuracy
* Memory-mapped files
* Used by: Spotify
* Read-heavy optimization

**6. PQ (Product Quantization)**
* Vector compression
* Divide vector into sub-vectors
* Quantize each sub-vector
* Significant memory reduction
* Small accuracy loss
* Used by: FAISS, Milvus

**7. SCANN (Scalable Nearest)**
* Google's algorithm
* Learned quantization
* Two-phase search
* High performance
* Open-source library;

:**Index Selection Criteria**
----
**Choose based on:**

**Dataset Size:**
* < 10K: Flat index
* 10K - 1M: IVF or HNSW
* 1M - 100M: HNSW + PQ
* 100M+: Distributed IVF + PQ

**Query Speed Requirements:**
* Real-time: HNSW
* Batch: IVF
* Moderate: IVF + filtering

**Accuracy Requirements:**
* High (>99%): HNSW, Flat
* Medium (95-99%): IVF
* Lower (<95%): LSH

**Memory Constraints:**
* Limited: PQ, SQ, IVF
* Abundant: HNSW, Flat

**Update Frequency:**
* High updates: HNSW
* Mostly reads: ANNOY, IVF
* Append-only: IVF;

}

partition "**DISTANCE METRICS & SIMILARITY**" {

:**Distance Metric Types**
----
**1. COSINE SIMILARITY**
* Measures angle between vectors
* Range: -1 to 1 (inclusive) or 0 to 1
* Formula: cos(θ) = (A·B) / (||A|| ||B||)
* Distance: 1 - cosine_similarity
* Use cases:
  - Text embeddings (most common)
  - Semantic similarity
  - Direction matters, not magnitude
* Normalized vectors
* Default for most LLM embeddings

**2. EUCLIDEAN DISTANCE (L2)**
* Straight-line distance
* Formula: sqrt(Σ(ai - bi)²)
* Range: 0 to infinity
* Use cases:
  - Image embeddings
  - Spatial data
  - When magnitude matters
* Sensitive to scale
* Most intuitive metric

**3. DOT PRODUCT (Inner Product)**
* Sum of element-wise products
* Formula: Σ(ai × bi)
* Range: negative infinity to positive infinity
* Higher = more similar
* Use cases:
  - Normalized embeddings
  - Fast computation
  - Neural network outputs
* Magnitude and direction matter
* Faster than cosine (no division)

**4. MANHATTAN DISTANCE (L1)**
* Sum of absolute differences
* Formula: Σ|ai - bi|
* Range: 0 to infinity
* Use cases:
  - Grid-like data
  - Sparse vectors
  - Robust to outliers
* Less common for embeddings

**5. HAMMING DISTANCE**
* Count of differing bits
* For binary vectors
* Very fast computation
* Use cases:
  - Binary embeddings
  - Hashing methods
  - Large-scale search
* Lower memory footprint;

:**Metric Selection Guide**
----
**Cosine Similarity:**
✓ Text embeddings (OpenAI, Cohere)
✓ Semantic search
✓ Document similarity
✓ Pre-normalized vectors

**Euclidean (L2):**
✓ Image embeddings
✓ Face recognition
✓ Object detection
✓ When scale matters

**Dot Product:**
✓ Fast similarity computation
✓ Already normalized vectors
✓ Neural network embeddings
✓ Real-time applications

**Performance Comparison:**
* Dot Product: Fastest
* Cosine: Fast (needs normalization)
* Euclidean: Moderate (sqrt operation)
* Manhattan: Moderate;

}

partition "**VECTOR INSERTION PROCESS**" {

:**1. Prepare Vectors**
----
* Generate embeddings:
  - Use embedding model
  - Text → Vector
  - Image → Vector
  - Audio → Vector
* Validate dimensions
* Normalize if needed
  (for cosine similarity)
* Prepare metadata
* Assign IDs;

:**2. Batch Preparation**
----
* Group vectors in batches:
  - Typical: 100-1000 per batch
  - Balance throughput vs memory
* Structure data:
  - Vector arrays
  - ID arrays
  - Metadata objects
* Validate data types
* Check batch limits;

:**3. Insert/Upsert Operation**
----
**Insert Methods:**

**Single Insert:**
* One vector at a time
* Simple but slow
* Use for real-time updates

**Batch Insert:**
* Multiple vectors together
* Much faster (10-100x)
* Recommended for bulk loads
* Parallel processing

**Upsert:**
* Update if exists, insert if not
* Based on ID
* Maintains consistency

**Actions:**
* Serialize vectors
* Write to storage
* Update indices
* Refresh metadata;

:**4. Index Building**
----
**Online Indexing:**
* Update index immediately
* Higher latency per insert
* Always queryable
* Use: Real-time systems

**Offline Indexing:**
* Build index after insertions
* Lower insert latency
* Temporary unavailability
* Use: Batch processing

**Incremental Indexing:**
* Periodic index updates
* Balance both approaches
* Background processing

**Actions:**
* Cluster assignment (IVF)
* Graph construction (HNSW)
* Tree building (ANNOY)
* Quantization (PQ);

:**5. Persistence**
----
* Write to disk
* Transaction logs
* Replication (if distributed)
* Snapshot creation
* Backup copies
* Verify integrity;

}

partition "**VECTOR SEARCH & RETRIEVAL**" {

:**1. Query Preparation**
----
* Receive query text/data
* Generate query embedding:
  - MUST use same model
  - Same dimensions
  - Same normalization
* Validate query vector
* Parse search parameters:
  - top_k (e.g., 5-20)
  - Filters (metadata)
  - Score threshold
  - Distance metric;

:**2. Query Routing**
----
**Distributed Systems:**
* Identify target shards/partitions
* Route based on:
  - Hash-based routing
  - Metadata filters
  - Load balancing
* Parallel query execution
* Aggregation of results;

:**3. Index Search**
----
**Algorithm Execution:**

**HNSW Search:**
* Start at top layer
* Greedy navigation
* Descend through layers
* Find nearest neighbors
* Return candidates

**IVF Search:**
* Find nearest centroids (nprobe)
* Search within clusters
* Aggregate results
* Sort by distance

**Flat Search:**
* Compare with all vectors
* Calculate distances
* Keep top-k
* Exact results

**Complexity:**
* HNSW: O(log n)
* IVF: O(nprobe × n/nlist)
* Flat: O(n);

:**4. Distance Calculation**
----
* Calculate similarity/distance:
  - Query vector vs candidates
  - Apply distance metric
  - Use SIMD/GPU acceleration
* Optimize calculations:
  - Pre-computed norms
  - Quantized comparisons
  - Approximate distances
* Sort by score;

:**5. Metadata Filtering**
----
**Pre-filtering:**
* Filter before vector search
* Reduce search space
* Faster but may miss results

**Post-filtering:**
* Filter after vector search
* More accurate
* May need more candidates

**Hybrid Filtering:**
* Combine both approaches
* Balance accuracy and speed

**Filter Types:**
* Exact match (category = "X")
* Range (date > "2024-01-01")
* Array contains (tags contains "AI")
* Geo-location (distance < 10km)
* Composite (multiple conditions);

:**6. Re-ranking (Optional)**
----
* Apply advanced scoring:
  - Combine vector + keyword scores
  - Weighted ranking
  - Cross-encoder models
  - Business logic scoring
* Adjust positions
* Apply diversity filters
* Remove duplicates;

:**7. Result Compilation**
----
* Collect top-k results:
  - Vector IDs
  - Similarity scores
  - Distances
* Fetch metadata
* Retrieve original content
  (if requested)
* Format response:
  - ID, score, metadata
  - Content (optional)
* Sort final results;

:**8. Response Return**
----
* Package results
* Return to client:
  - Result array
  - Scores/distances
  - Metadata
  - Query metadata
    (time taken, vectors scanned)
* Log query
* Update metrics;

}

partition "**OPTIMIZATION & PERFORMANCE**" {

:**Performance Tuning**
----
**Index Optimization:**
* Tune index parameters:
  - HNSW: M, efConstruction, efSearch
  - IVF: nlist, nprobe
* Balance accuracy vs speed
* A/B test configurations
* Benchmark regularly

**Quantization:**
* Reduce vector precision:
  - Float32 → Float16 (2x compression)
  - Float32 → Int8 (4x compression)
* Product Quantization:
  - 8-32x compression
  - Small accuracy loss
* Binary quantization:
  - 32x compression
  - Faster computation

**Caching:**
* Cache frequent queries
* Cache query embeddings
* Cache hot vectors
* LRU/LFU policies
* Distributed caching

**Hardware Optimization:**
* GPU acceleration
* SIMD instructions
* Multi-threading
* Memory-mapped files
* NVMe SSDs for storage;

:**Benchmark Metrics**
----
**Accuracy Metrics:**
* Recall@k: % of true neighbors found
* Precision@k: % relevant in results
* nDCG: Ranking quality
* Mean Average Precision (MAP)

**Performance Metrics:**
* QPS (Queries Per Second)
* Latency: p50, p95, p99
* Throughput (vectors/sec)
* Index build time
* Memory usage
* Disk usage

**Common Benchmarks:**
* ANN-Benchmarks.com
* Datasets: SIFT, GIST, Deep1B
* Metrics: Recall vs QPS curves

**Typical Performance:**
* HNSW: 10K-100K QPS
* IVF+PQ: 1K-10K QPS
* Flat: 100-1K QPS
* Latency: 1-50ms;

}

partition "**ADVANCED FEATURES**" {

:**Hybrid Search**
----
* Combine vector + keyword search:
  - Dense retrieval (vectors)
  - Sparse retrieval (BM25, TF-IDF)
* Fusion strategies:
  - Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)
  - Weighted combination
  - Score normalization
* Best for: Document search
* Higher accuracy
* Supported: Weaviate, Elasticsearch;

:**Filtered Search**
----
* Apply metadata filters
* Efficient filtering methods:
  - Inverted indices
  - Bitmap indices
  - Hash tables
* Reduce search space
* Maintain performance;

:**Multi-vector Search**
----
* Multiple vectors per document:
  - Different aspects
  - Multi-modal (text + image)
  - Chunks of same document
* Late interaction models
* ColBERT-style search;

:**Updates & Deletes**
----
* Update vectors:
  - Upsert by ID
  - Version control
  - Atomic operations
* Delete vectors:
  - Mark as deleted (soft delete)
  - Remove from index
  - Garbage collection
  - Compaction
* Maintain consistency;

:**Replication & HA**
----
* Data replication:
  - Master-replica
  - Multi-master
  - Quorum-based
* Failover mechanisms
* Load balancing
* Geographic distribution
* Consistency models:
  - Strong consistency
  - Eventual consistency;

}

:**Vector Database Ready**;

